// aug13l hz1 divergence_000000 (2026-08-14): the FIRST LIVE WITNESS of
// the recursion ruling's DOCUMENTED KNOWN RESIDUAL (2026-08-13,
// CLAUDE.md): changed-args-same-selection-profile with a const
// terminal — kernel fires per delivery, interp quiet.
//
// f(x-1) over x = iter([0,2,0,4]): every derivation lands on value 8.
// INTERP (quiet, = strict select + recursion ruling composed): the
// outer dispatch's scrutinee changes (-1/1/-1/3) but the selection
// doesn't; the arm body `8 - f(n % 1)` re-derives, but the INNER
// call's arg is 0 every time — unchanged inputs, not an event
// (recursion ruling) — so the inner rides quiet, the subtraction has
// no fired operand, the arm produces nothing, and the select is
// quiet. One emission total (the init derivation).
// KERNEL (fires): the root call's derivation-changed bit is TRUE
// (root args changed), and the damping doesn't reach the
// const-terminal return fold — the result re-fires 8 per delivery.
//
// The cousin of the PENDING tail-zero-iteration ruling (the written
// tail-spine rule's "a loop bound is a control dependence whose new
// value is otherwise lost" argues FIRE; Eric's 2026-08-13 reading of
// the strict rule argued QUIET). Same fork, non-tail face. AWAITING
// the ruling — do not fix either engine unilaterally.
{let x = array::iter([i64:0, i64:2, i64:0, i64:4]); let rec f = |n: i64| -> i64 select n {i64:0 => i64:0, _ => i64:8 - f(n % i64:1)}; f(x - i64:1)}
